{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"{\"additions\":9,\"author\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOBsUTKg\",\"is_bot\":false,\"login\":\"chuoer47\",\"name\":\"辍二\"},\"body\":\"## Problem\\n\\nCloses #6.\\n\\nOn NVIDIA GPUs, Triton 3.1 uses TF32 by default for FP32 `tl.dot` operations, while the PyTorch reference path in this tutorial has TF32 disabled (`torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 == False`). The precision mismatch keeps the forward pass within tolerance, but the error accumulates and is amplified through the backward kernels, producing gradient errors up to ~0.72 and failing `assert_close(atol=5e-3)`.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\nAdd `input_precision=\\\"ieee\\\"` to every fp32 `tl.dot` in both the forward and backward kernels (9 call sites) so the Triton matmuls match the PyTorch reference precision:\\n\\n- `_attn_fwd_inner`: `S = tl.dot(Q, K_T, ...)`, `O = tl.dot(P, V, acc=O, ...)`\\n- `_attn_backward_KV`: `S_T`, `dLdV`, `dLdP_T`, `dLdK`\\n- `_attn_backward_Q`: `S`, `dLdP`, `dLdQ`\\n\\nThis is an explicit, in-kernel fix (rather than relying on the `TRITON_F32_DEFAULT=ieee` env var), so the tests pass regardless of the user's default Triton FP32 precision.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\nEnvironment: Triton 3.1.0, PyTorch 2.5.1+cu124, RTX 4090, `torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32=False`, fresh Triton cache.\\n\\nAll 4 `test_flashattention_kernel` configurations pass forward + backward:\\n\\n| config (B, H, N, Dh) | fwd | bwd |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| (1, 1, 128, 32)   | passed | passed |\\n| (1, 1, 128, 64)   | passed | passed |\\n| (1, 1, 128, 128)  | passed | passed |\\n| (32, 8, 69, 128)  | passed | passed |\\n\\nMax gradient diffs (config 1, 1, 128, 32), down from ~0.72 before the fix:\\n\\n```\\nfwd max diff: 1.37e-06\\ndQ max diff: 3.43e-07\\ndK max diff: 4.62e-07\\ndV max diff: 1.79e-07\\n```\\n\\nAll diffs are now below 1e-6, consistent with the IEEE recompilation results reported in the issue.\\n\",\"deletions\":9,\"files\":[{\"path\":\"09_flash_attention/flash_attention.py\",\"additi